# RIO

An operating system for the RISC-V architecture.

## Installation (Arch Linux)

To compile and run RIO on Arch Linux, install the RISC-V bare-metal toolchain, GNU Make, and QEMU:

```bash
sudo pacman -S base-devel riscv64-elf-gcc riscv64-elf-binutils qemu-system-riscv
```

Package details:
- `base-devel`: Provides GNU `make` and essential compilation utilities.
- `riscv64-elf-gcc`: GCC cross-compiler targeting 64-bit bare-metal RISC-V (`riscv64-elf-gcc`).
- `riscv64-elf-binutils`: Assembler and binary utilities for RISC-V (`riscv64-elf-as`).
- `qemu-system-riscv`: QEMU system emulator for RISC-V, providing `qemu-system-riscv64`.

## Makefile Guide

The provided Makefile contains three primary targets:

- `make` or `make build`:
  Compiles `src/kernel.c` with freestanding settings, assembles `src/boot.s`, and links the objects with `linker.ld` into `rio.elf`.

  ```bash
  make build
  ```

- `make run`:
  Runs the compiled kernel binary (`rio.elf`) inside QEMU using the RISC-V virt machine with direct serial console output.

  ```bash
  make run
  ```

  To exit QEMU, press `Ctrl+A` followed by `X`.

- `make clean`:
  Deletes the generated kernel binary (`rio.elf`) and object files in `bin/`.

  ```bash
  make clean
  ```

## Project Structure

```text
rio/
├── bin/           # Output directory for compiled object files (.o)
├── src/
│   ├── boot.s     # Assembly startup code (entry point, stack setup, BSS clear)
│   ├── kernel.c   # Main kernel entry point (kmain)
│   ├── kprint.c   # Kernel printing functions and UART communication
│   ├── kprint.h   # Header declarations for kernel printing functions
│   └── ktypes.h   # Kernel type definitions, limits, and status codes
├── LICENSE        # GNU General Public License v3
├── linker.ld      # Linker script mapping sections to the RAM base address
├── Makefile       # Build rules and QEMU execution targets
└── README         # Project documentation and setup instructions
```
